<Layout title="Platform — Chapter Publishing" description="A contract-driven publishing platform for editorial state, offline clients, and release evidence."><section class="page-hero shell"><p class="eyebrow">Platform</p><h1>One editorial contract, multiple ways to work.</h1><p>The platform is designed around explicit revision state, portable interfaces, and clients that can carry work across desktop, mobile, and offline boundaries.</p></section><section class="section shell card-grid"><article><span>Contracts</span><h2>State that tools can share</h2><p>Typed interfaces give authors, clients, and services one vocabulary for chapters, revisions, reviews, and releases.</p></article><article><span>Clients</span><h2>Work where the chapter is</h2><p>Flutter and Rust desktop clients are part of the intended product surface, with current availability tracked in the repositories.</p></article><article><span>Delivery</span><h2>Evidence around the release</h2><p>Private operational telemetry can help explain a publishing session without turning the public site into a tracking surface.</p></article></section></Layout>
